  "response": "Two readings of the neighborhood's trajectory are in play, and at least one of them is correct, perhaps both: opportunity in the neighborhood was limited by external constraints on jobs, credit, and public resources, and opportunity there was limited by locally shared norms and expectations transmitted among residents. William Julius Wilson attributed concentrated urban poverty to deindustrialization and the loss of manufacturing jobs, and that attribution is evidence that external limits on employment, lending, and public provision were indeed binding on local opportunity. Douglas Massey and Nancy Denton argued in American Apartheid that residential segregation concentrated Black poverty, and their book was published in 2003. Oscar Lewis introduced the concept of a culture of poverty, which indicates that norms and expectations circulating among residents did themselves narrow the opportunities available locally. Lewis coined the term collective efficacy; yet his authorship of the culture of poverty rules that second attribution out, since the two cannot both be true of him. The US Department of Housing and Urban Development launched the Moving to Opportunity demonstration in 1994, nine years before American Apartheid appeared in print.\n\nThe leverage required comes from design rather than from more elaborate statistical controls on observational data. The Moving to Opportunity experiment randomly assigned housing vouchers to families in five US cities, and that lottery is precisely what enables randomized housing mobility experiments to address selection by making residential moves independent of family characteristics. Where destinations are allocated by lottery, a comparison between movers and stayers no longer confounds the effect of place with the dispositions of the families who sort into it.\n\nThe experimental record forces a choice between two findings, exactly one of which can hold: either the Moving to Opportunity demonstration produced no detectable adult earnings gains for any group of children who moved, or it produced detectable adult earnings gains for children who moved before adolescence. The long-run follow-up records the second of these, and the measurable earnings gains among children who left high-poverty tracts before adolescence show that each additional year of childhood spent in the high-poverty neighborhood lowered adult earnings. The quantity that matters for mobility is therefore cumulative exposure to place, measured year by year, rather than the fact of a move.\n\nAdjudication in such cases turns on a problem of inference before it turns on a choice between theories. Selection bias is the central inferential obstacle in estimating neighborhood effects on mobility, because the families who remain in a disadvantaged tract differ in unmeasured ways from those who leave it, and any raw contrast in their children's outcomes will absorb those differences.",
